WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
YURI GRINSHTEYN: It's more
important than ever for teams

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:04.710
to take ownership of the
reliability of their services.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.890
This is especially true in
distributed environments,

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:08.720
like an e-commerce
website that depends

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.730
on services like
inventory, shopping

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:12.320
cart, and notifications.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.310
Today, we'll look at how to
use data from a service mesh

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:18.560
to understand traffic,
error rates, and latencies

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.540
in microservice applications
with Stackdriver.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.960
This is "The Stack Doctor."

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.320
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.940
Developers use microservices
to architect for portability,

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:33.950
scale, and decoupling.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.070
This presents some challenges
with operations and management.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:39.350
You have to manage all
of the various services

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:41.880
and understand the
interactions between them.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.940
This is where a
service mesh comes in.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.640
Let's take a look at how a
service mesh and Stackdriver

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.500
can work together to
help us understand

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.590
what's going on in a
distributed application.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:53.600
To start, we need services.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.050
We'll use this application.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.600
It's a web store that
sells really cool products.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.560
It also happens to be composed
of a number of microservices

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.486
written in a variety
of languages.

00:01:02.486 --> 00:01:04.069
You can take a look
at it for yourself

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:06.290
in this GitHub repository.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.370
In a complex
environment like this,

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:11.160
how can we go about
measuring reliability?

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.890
This is where a service
mesh is going to help us.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.430
But first, what
is a service mesh?

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:18.763
According to the Istio
open source project,

00:01:18.763 --> 00:01:20.180
a service mesh is
used to describe

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.130
the network of
microservices that

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:25.520
make up distributed applications
and the interactions

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:26.660
between them.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.330
The real value of a
service mesh like Istio

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:32.280
is that it enhances the
security, reliability,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.040
and observability
of services, which

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.550
are not necessarily easy to
do in Kubernetes without it.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:38.805
What does that mean for us?

00:01:38.805 --> 00:01:40.430
Well, let's take a
look at what happens

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:42.710
when we have our microservices
application managed

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:43.820
by a service mesh.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.860
First, let's create
a GKE cluster

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.977
with Istio already
installed on it.

00:01:47.977 --> 00:01:50.060
Here's the command we'll
use to create the cluster

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:51.860
and enable Istio.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.230
This will automatically set
up the Stackdriver adapter

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:58.177
to send metrics, logs,
and traces to Stackdriver.

00:01:58.177 --> 00:01:59.760
Now that the cluster's
up and running,

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.360
let's deploy our
microservices application.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.240
Here are all the
services that it creates.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.509
These services are instrumented
with the proxy sidecar.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:10.580
The proxies send
telemetry information

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:13.640
to a component called Mixer,
which in turn sends it

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:14.840
to Stackdriver.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:17.810
Let's go there and see what
new information is available.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.580
We'll start in the
metrics explorer.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.200
If we search for
Istio, we'll see

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:24.200
all of the new metrics that
are created by Istio for all

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.775
of the services in the mesh.

00:02:25.775 --> 00:02:27.650
The first thing we want
to do is to configure

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.260
alerting using these metrics
so that we can find out

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:33.020
if any of our services have
issues with performance

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:34.740
or availability.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.030
First, let's create an alerting
policy for front-end latency.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.190
We'll use the server
response latency metric

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:43.730
filtered by destination service
name for just our front end,

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.860
and further filter
by response code

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:49.460
to just measure latency
for successful requests.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.350
Now let's create
another alerting policy

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:53.120
for our front end availability.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:56.240
This time, we'll use the
server request count metric.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.300
We'll also filter by destination
service name for the front end,

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.580
and then further filter
it to only count responses

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.680
that do not return a success.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:04.698
And there we go.

00:03:04.698 --> 00:03:06.740
Now we're using information
from the service mesh

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:08.810
to let us know when
our front end service

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:13.070
experience is in degradation in
either latency or availability.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:16.010
But what happens when we do get
an alert that there's an issue

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:17.570
and our service is degraded?

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.610
If the problem has
to do with latency,

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:21.730
we'd want to start by
looking at tracing.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:23.920
Well, the good news is that
the Stackdriver adapter

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.730
in the service mesh is not
just sending metric data,

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.143
it's also exporting traces.

00:03:29.143 --> 00:03:31.310
We don't need to do anything
to instrument our code.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.000
The service mesh
trace has serviced

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.415
the service calls for us and
sends the data to Stackdriver.

00:03:36.415 --> 00:03:37.790
On the other hand,
if the problem

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.133
has to do with
availability or errors,

00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:42.050
we would want to have
easy access to debugging

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:43.760
information, like logs.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.688
The service mesh is also
exporting logs to Stackdriver.

00:03:46.688 --> 00:03:48.980
We can look at Kubernetes
container logs for the proxy,

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:51.140
for example, to see a
record of all requests.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:53.060
If there are any issues
we need to debug,

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.197
this can be a great
source of information.

00:03:55.197 --> 00:03:57.530
So thanks to the data being
exported by the service mesh

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:00.230
to Stackdriver, we've
configured alerting and service

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:03.470
availability and performance,
looked at latency with traces,

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:04.645
and dug into logs.

00:04:04.645 --> 00:04:06.020
Come back next
time, when we look

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:09.380
at improving observability even
further with custom metrics.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:10.790
That's it for this episode.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:12.170
This is "The Stack Doctor."

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:13.730
Stay healthy out there.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:17.380
[MUSIC PLAYING]

